Let's implement it.

Rules:
- R1: items is an array of {id, size}. slotSize is a positive integer.
- R2: Sort by size in descending order; for the same size, by id in ascending order (string comparison).
- R3: First-fit in order of slot creation.
- R4: An array of slots (arrays of ids) in creation order.
- R5: When size > slotSize → throw a RangeError. The message should include the id of the first item that is too big in placement order (after R2 sorting). And "no others" — meaning only that id should be named. For example: `RangeError: item "b" (size 7) exceeds slotSize 6` — as long as it contains "b" and no other id, it's fine.
- R6: Do not mutate the input — sort a copy.
- R7: Empty → [].

Implementation:

```js
export function packSlots(items, slotSize) {
	const order = items
		.map((item, index) => ({ item, index }))
		.sort((x, y) => {
			if (y.item.size !== x.item.size) return y.item.size - x.item.size;
			return x.item.id < y.item.id ? -1 : x.item.id > y.item.id ? 1 : 0;
		});

	let toThrow = null;
	for (const { item } of order) {
		if (item.size > slotSize) {
			if (toThrow === null) toThrow = item;
			break; // only the first in placement order
		}
	}
	if (toThrow) {
		throw new RangeError(`item "${toThrow.id}" has size ${toThrow.size}, which is greater than slot size ${slotSize}`);
	}

	const slots = [];
	for (const { item } of order) {
		let placed = false;
		for (const slot of slots) {
			if (slot[0] >= item.size) {
				slot[0] -= item.size;
				slot[1].push(item.id);
				placed = true;
				break;
			}
		}
		if (!placed) {
			slots.push([slotSize - item.size, [item.id]]);
		}
	}
	return slots.map((s) => s[1]);
}
```

The message should only mention that id, and must not include any other id. `item "b" ...` contains "b". Good.

Wait — a subtle point about R5: "When multiple items are too big, the message should indicate the first in placement order (R2), and no others." My approach: first check for oversized items in placement order, throw on the first, and break. Good.

Also, the sort needs to be stable enough: since we're comparing by size descending, then id ascending, it's a total order (ids are probably unique; if ids are duplicated, any order is fine).

